# Ahiru - file manager

*Ahiru* is Japanese for a (white) duck - a nod to Yazi, the Chinese duck
it takes after. It was called UP-File until version 0.2b2.

A Ranger/Yazi-style file manager for the classic Amiga (AmigaOS 2.04 and
up), in text mode: on a 4, 8 or 16 colour screen of its own, in a window
on Workbench, or **inside the terminal** it was started from (a CTerm tab
or pane, or any Amiga console window) - see "Three ways to show" below.

Three columns, Ranger's 1:3:4: the **parent** directory on the left, the
**current** one in the middle, and a **preview** on the right of whatever
the cursor is on:

- a directory shows its contents;
- text shows its first screen, **ANSI art shows in colour**;
- an executable shows its hunk count and `$VER:` string;
- an IFF picture shows its size, colours and mode (HAM, EHB), an 8SVX
  sound its rate and length;
- a ProTracker module shows its title, patterns and sample names;
- an LhA archive lists its members (header levels 0, 1 and 2), an LZX or
  Zip archive its member count and unpacked size;
- an ADF disk image shows its filesystem and volume name;
- anything else shows as hex.

The preview is worked out only when the keyboard is idle, so holding a
key down over a slow disk never reads a file per row.

## Keys

Every key is configurable: `PROGDIR:Ahiru.keys` lists them, one a line
(`gg top`, `<Up> up`, `gs go SYS:`, `q none` to take a key away). Ahiru
writes the file with all its built-in keys the first time it starts;
edit it and start Ahiru again. The help (`?`) and the panels after a
prefix key are made from it, so they always show the keys as they are.
A line Ahiru does not understand is said in red at start; the others
still work. The built-in keys:

vi keys, as in Ranger. A prefix key (`g`, `y`, `d`, `p`, `c`, `u`, `z`,
`o`, `=`) opens a small panel at the bottom listing what can follow;
`m` and `'` show the bookmarks and take a key of their own.

| Key | Action |
|---|---|
| `j` `k` / Up Down | move (`J` `K` half a page, Shift+Up/Down a page) |
| `h` / Left | parent directory (at a root: the volume list) |
| `l` / Right / Enter | enter a directory, an archive (lha, lzx, zip) or a disk image (ADF, ISO; a DMS file is unpacked to an ADF first, asks), or open a file by type: run a program (asks), read text/ANSI, show pictures, sounds and guides in MultiView |
| `gg` `G` | top, bottom |
| `i` | view the file full screen (text, ANSI art, hex; `x` flips to hex) |
| `E` | edit (EDITOR in the config, else `ENV:EDITOR`, else `C:Ed`); Sumi, the editor that lives in the terminal, gets a console window as big as Ahiru's screen, or the terminal pane when Ahiru runs inside one |
| Space | mark and move on; `v` inverts the marks, `uv` clears them |
| `yy` / `dd` | yank (copy) / cut the marked set, or the entry under the cursor |
| `pp` / `po` | paste here / paste, overwriting everything |
| `dD` / Del | delete (asks; delete-protected files ask again) |
| `cw` `a` `A` `I` | rename: empty, cursor at the extension, at the end, at the start |
| `M` | make a directory |
| `x` | unpack the marked archives (or the one under the cursor) here, with no outside unpacker (asks) |
| `/` `n` `N` | search, next, previous |
| `f` | filter the listing (empty or Esc clears) |
| `on` `os` `ot` `oe` `or` | sort by name, size, time, extension; reverse (capitals sort reversed) |
| `zh` `zp` `zc` | show/hide `.info` icons, preview column on/off, cycle 4/8/16 colours |
| `gs` `gc` `gl` `gd` `ge` `gt` `gr` | go to SYS: C: LIBS: DEVS: ENV: T: RAM: |
| `gv` `g/` `gh` | the volume list, this volume's root, home (START) |
| `m` + key, `'` + key | bookmark this directory, jump to a bookmark |
| `cd` | go to a typed path (a file's path opens its drawer on it) |
| `:` | command line: `mkdir NAME`, `touch NAME`, `cd PATH`, `shell COMMAND`, `rename NEW` (the entry under the cursor; without NEW it asks), `q` or `quit` |
| `!` / `S` | run a command here (output in a console) / open a shell here |
| `R` / F5 | reload |
| `pk` | pack the marked (or the entry under the cursor) into a new archive here |
| `cr` | rename by pattern (`*.JPG` to `*.jpg`): the marked, else all, with a preview of old -> new first |
| `=c` / `=s` | compare with the next tab's folder (marks what differs) / make the next tab's folder like this one |
| `=m` | MD5 and CRC-32 of the marked files (Return saves a `.md5`); on a `.md5` or `.sfv` file: check the files it lists |
| `=d` | an `.adf` into a drive (`DA0:`) and in; again, or inside the drive: out |
| `?` / Help | the key list |
| `q` | quit |

Icons travel with their files: copying, moving, renaming or deleting
`Foo` takes `Foo.info` along whether icons are shown or not.

The cursor position is remembered per directory, so going back into a
drawer puts you where you were.

## Settings

`PROGDIR:Ahiru.config`, written on quit; unknown lines are ignored:

```
COLORS 8              ; 4, 8 or 16
FONT topaz.font/8     ; any fixed-width font
HIDEINFO yes          ; hide .info icons
PREVIEW yes           ; the third column
SORT name             ; name, size, date or ext
REVERSE no
START SYS:            ; where gh goes
SAVEDIR yes           ; start where you quit (LASTDIR)
EDITOR                ; empty: ENV:EDITOR, then C:Ed (C:Sumi suggested)
EDITORCONSOLE no      ; yes: the editor needs a console (Sumi is known as one)
HOTKEY ctrl alt a     ; (2.0+) brings Ahiru up in the drawer Workbench has active
CXPOPUP yes           ; no: start hidden and wait for the hotkey; q then only hides
VERIFY no             ; yes: every copy is read back and compared before it is kept
LINEMODE size         ; after the names: size, date, bits or none
OPENWITH mod Play     ; offered by O for files ending .mod (* = any)
MODE auto             ; auto, screen, window or terminal (see below)
WINDOW 40 30 680 400  ; the window's place and size (window mode), kept
```

From Workbench, the icon's tool types `HOTKEY` and `CXPOPUP` win over
the file.

Bookmarks live in `PROGDIR:Ahiru.marks`, one per line (`a DH0:Work`).

The palettes: **16** is the classic ANSI sixteen; **8** is the ANSI eight
with the blue lifted so directories read on black; **4** is black, grey,
sky blue and amber. Colours by kind: directories blue, programs green,
archives red, pictures yellow, sounds magenta, source cyan, icons dim.
These come from **`ENVARC:FileColours`**, the file csh's `ls` reads too
(written with these defaults when missing), so a file looks the same in
Ahiru as in `ls`: change a kind's colour there, or put an ending into a
kind (`.foo picture`), and both follow. Links, scripts (s bit) and hidden
files (h bit) have colours of their own.

## Usage

```
Ahiru [PATH] [SCREEN | WINDOW | TERMINAL]
```

## Three ways to show

- **Screen** - a screen of its own. It copies the Workbench's display
  mode and size; if that mode will not take the depth, a plain hires
  screen is used; if no screen can be opened, a window on Workbench.
- **Window** - an ordinary window on Workbench: moved, sized and closed
  as any, in Workbench's colours. Its place and size are kept
  (`WINDOW` in the config).
- **Terminal** - inside the terminal Ahiru was started from, the way
  ranger and yazi run inside a terminal on Linux: no window or screen of
  its own, it draws in the CTerm tab or pane (or the console window) it
  was typed in. When it quits, the terminal is as it was - in CTerm its
  screens do not end up in the history.
  - In **CTerm**: CTerm's theme colours, the mouse (click, double-click,
    the wheel), picture previews as real pictures (sixel), and it follows
    the pane's size (Left Amiga+Return beside it gives a Shell next to
    Ahiru).
  - In **any other console** (CON:, KingCON, ...): Workbench's colours,
    no mouse, no pictures; the screen is cleared when it quits.
  - Needs **`LIBS:tui.library` 0.3 or newer**, the terminal programs'
    shared toolkit (the one Sumi, Maki and Markdown use; built in
    the AmigaLibs repo's `tui/`, one file per processor). Without it Ahiru
    says so and opens on its screen instead.
  - `!` (a Shell command), `S` (a Shell) and the editor run *in the
    terminal* while Ahiru waits, then Ahiru comes back - as in ranger.
  - No hotkey there: a second Ahiru in a terminal does not pop up the
    first one.

**MODE auto** (the start): inside the terminal when Ahiru is started
from a Shell in CTerm, otherwise its own screen. `MODE screen`, `window`
or `terminal` in the config fixes one; the words `SCREEN`, `WINDOW` and
`TERMINAL` after `Ahiru` choose for that start only. `TERMINAL` from a
Workbench icon (there is no console) is the screen.

## Building

C, cross-compiled with Bebbo's `m68k-amigaos-gcc` (libnix):

```
make          # builds Ahiru (the core is the folder above: CFILE=..)
```

Terminal mode is drawn by tui.library (`src/term.c` turns its events into
Ahiru's); its headers come from `TUI=../../AmigaLibs/tui`.

The work is done by the core in the CFile repo, which CFile uses too:
`core/` reads the formats (lha, lzx, zip, ADF, ISO, DMS, IFF, ANSI,
natural sort, file kinds) and `fs/` does folders, the volume list,
archives and disk images as folders, copy, move, delete, rename and new
folder with the safety rules (a file is written beside the old one and
swapped in only when whole, a move deletes only what arrived, links stay
links, icons travel with their files, a newer version is never replaced
without asking). Ahiru is the screen and the keys; `src/fe.c` answers
the core's questions and draws its progress bar. The core has its own
tests in the CFile repo (`make test`, `tests/fs.sh`); see
`CFile/docs/core.md`.

## Tabs, visual mode, finding, the trashcan, sound

- **Tabs**: `t` opens a new tab where you are, `]` and `[` go to the next
  and the one before, `1`-`9` go straight to one, Ctrl-W closes it. The
  title row shows them: ` 1 [2] 3`.
- **Visual mode**: `V`, then move: everything between where you started
  and the cursor is marked. `V` or Esc ends it, the marks stay.
- **Bulk rename**: `cb` puts the marked names (or all of them) in your
  editor, one a line; change them, save, and they are renamed - icons
  along, and two names can swap.
- **Find**: `s` finds names in this folder and every one below (a part of
  the name, or an AmigaDOS pattern such as `#?.mod`); Ctrl-S finds files
  holding a text. The hits show as a list of their own: preview, copy,
  delete, Enter as anywhere. `h` or `R` goes back to the folder.
- **The trashcan**: `dt` moves to the volume's Trashcan drawer, as
  Workbench has it; a disk without one gets one, with the system's
  trashcan icon. `dD` still deletes for good.
- **Sound**: `P` plays the module or 8SVX sound under the cursor in the
  background while you go on; `P` again stops it.

## Background copies, the clipboard, open with, and more

- **Background copies**: a paste between folders on disks runs in a
  process of its own; the status row counts it up and you go on. `w`
  shows it (`x` there stops it). A question it has (a name already
  there) comes up as usual. While it runs, the other file operations
  wait for it. Pastes into or out of archives stay in the front.
- **The clipboard**: `cc` copies the path, `cf` the name, `cF` the folder.
- **Open with**: `O` offers the program the file's icon names, the
  `OPENWITH ext program` lines of `Ahiru.config`, MultiView, the editor,
  or running it; a digit or Return picks.
- **Jump by habit**: `Z` and a part of a name goes to the folder used
  most (and lately) that holds it; several: a digit picks. Remembered in
  `PROGDIR:Ahiru.places`.
- **Live filter**: `f` narrows the list as you type; Esc gives it back.
- **Line mode**: `L` shows sizes, dates, protection bits or nothing after
  the names (`LINEMODE` in the config).
- **Mouse**: a click selects, a second quick one opens; a click in the
  parent column goes up onto it (from find's hits: back to the folder
  searched, as `h`), in a folder's preview goes into it; the
  wheel scrolls.
- **Colours**: `PROGDIR:Ahiru.theme` holds the palette and which colour
  draws what, for 4, 8 and 16 colours; written when missing. The file
  kinds' colours in `ENVARC:FileColours` (csh's, `KIND CTERM,PLAIN`) win
  where it gives one: its first number on Ahiru's own screen and in CTerm
  (ANSI colours, 90-97 bright; grey is the theme's dim), its second in a
  Workbench window or a plain console (30-33 = Workbench's pens 0-3).
- **ARexx**: the port `AHIRU` - `GO path`, `GET PATH|NAME|FILE|MARKED`,
  `ACTION name [argument]` (any action of Ahiru.keys), `REFRESH`, `QUIT`.
  While Ahiru is hidden or waits in a question, `GET` is answered,
  `QUIT` quits when hidden, and the rest return 5 (busy).
- **Your own commands**: a line in `Ahiru.keys` binds a key to `run`
  (the output in a console, waits) or `launch` (starts it and goes on):
  `<F2> run Echo {file}`, `<F3> launch SYS:Utilities/MultiView {file}`.
  `{file}` `{name}` `{dir}` `{marked}` `{other}` (the next tab's folder)
  are filled in, quoted.

## Archives and disk images

Enter on an lha, lzx or zip archive, an ADF or ISO image opens it like a
folder: walk around in it, view (`i`) and edit (`E`) its files (an edit
goes back in when saved), yank out of it and paste into it, rename and
delete inside it. Changes to lha, lzx and zip archives use C:lha, C:lzx
and C:zip on a copy, which Ahiru reads back and checks before it
replaces the archive; ADF images are written by Ahiru itself. A typed
path (`cd`) or a bookmark can point inside an archive.

For testing on an emulator without a keyboard (over wasabi):

- `KEYS=...` plays keys at start: `|` is Return, `~` Esc, `{` `}` Left
  and Right, `_` Space.
- `LOG` writes each startup stage, with the free memory, to `RAM:Ahiru.log` and, once the
  played keys are used up, the whole screen as text to
  `RAM:Ahiru.grid` (rows of characters, then the foreground and
  background pen of every cell). A screen grab of a planar screen
  drops pixel columns from letters; the grid is exact.
- Ctrl-C quits from anywhere, the viewer and prompts included.

Ahiru uses no C stdio at all (its own `fmt()` replaces `sprintf`):
libnix's stdio setup returns 20 before `main` when the program is
started without a console, as wasabi does.
